+{
+  "spec": "mathbrain-draft-v1",
+  "author_agent": "w344",
+  "bundle_slug": "w344-open-problem-c0087-checked-partials-20261010",
+  "entries": [
+    {
+      "key": "kruskal-sublevel-intrinsic-diameter-lower-transfer",
+      "namespace": "probability.minimum_spanning_trees",
+      "logical_name": "kruskal-sublevel-intrinsic-diameter-lower-transfer",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Sublevel component intrinsic diameter is a lower bound for MST diameter",
+      "statement": "Let G be a finite connected graph with distinct edge weights, T its minimum spanning tree, and G_p its subgraph of edges of weight at most p. For each component C of G_p, the restriction T to C is a spanning tree and the T path between vertices of C stays in C. Therefore max_C diam_intrinsic(C)<=diam(T). A probabilistic lower bound on sublevel intrinsic component diameter transfers directly to the MST; this gives no MST upper bound or high-dimensional cluster estimate by itself.",
+      "proof_text": "Open Problem 16.3 in the same cached source asks for MST diameter of order V^(1/3) on high-dimensional tori and the hypercube. The prior proposed reduction lacked complete geometric hypotheses. We prove only its deterministic lower-bound step.\n\nLet G be a finite connected graph with distinct edge weights, T its MST, and G_p the graph of edges with weight <=p. Kruskal's algorithm has, by level p, selected a spanning tree inside every component C of G_p. These selected edges remain in T. Hence the unique T path between any u,v in C lies inside C, and its length is at least their shortest-path distance in C. Consequently max_C diam_intrinsic(C)<=diam(T). Any probabilistic lower bound for the left side transfers directly to the MST diameter without a universality assumption.\n\nThe reverse inequality fails: on K_n give the edges of a Hamilton path the n-1 smallest weights and every other edge larger distinct weights. Kruskal selects exactly that path, so diam(T)=n-1. At a threshold including every edge, G_p=K_n has intrinsic diameter 1. Thus intrinsic cluster-diameter control at one level gives no MST upper bound. This does not refute high-dimensional scaling. The script checks all 38 connected labeled four-vertex graphs at every selected threshold and exact K_4,K_8,K_16 path examples. Full torus/hypercube bounds remain unresolved in this packet.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "Finite connected graph",
+        "Distinct edge weights",
+        "Graph distance counts edges"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-w344-open-problems-20261010:kruskal-sublevel-intrinsic-diameter-lower-transfer",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w344/PROOFS.md",
+        "revision": "2026-10-10 worker result; proof; independent audit pending w346; novelty unverified",
+        "license": "owner-own-work (campaign proof and attributed mathematical source audit)",
+        "excerpt": "Let G be a finite connected graph with distinct edge weights, T its minimum spanning tree, and G_p"
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "key": "complete-graph-path-mst-reverses-no-diameter-upper-bound",
+      "namespace": "probability.minimum_spanning_trees",
+      "logical_name": "complete-graph-path-mst-reverses-no-diameter-upper-bound",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Small sublevel graph diameter need not bound MST diameter",
+      "statement": "For each n>=3 there is a distinct-weight assignment on K_n such that the MST is a Hamilton path of diameter n-1, while a sublevel including all edges has intrinsic diameter 1. Assign the n-1 path edges the smallest weights and every other edge larger weights. Hence a cluster intrinsic-diameter bound at one threshold does not imply the reverse MST-diameter inequality. This refutes that generic upper-bound route, not torus or hypercube scaling.",
+      "proof_text": "Open Problem 16.3 in the same cached source asks for MST diameter of order V^(1/3) on high-dimensional tori and the hypercube. The prior proposed reduction lacked complete geometric hypotheses. We prove only its deterministic lower-bound step.\n\nLet G be a finite connected graph with distinct edge weights, T its MST, and G_p the graph of edges with weight <=p. Kruskal's algorithm has, by level p, selected a spanning tree inside every component C of G_p. These selected edges remain in T. Hence the unique T path between any u,v in C lies inside C, and its length is at least their shortest-path distance in C. Consequently max_C diam_intrinsic(C)<=diam(T). Any probabilistic lower bound for the left side transfers directly to the MST diameter without a universality assumption.\n\nThe reverse inequality fails: on K_n give the edges of a Hamilton path the n-1 smallest weights and every other edge larger distinct weights. Kruskal selects exactly that path, so diam(T)=n-1. At a threshold including every edge, G_p=K_n has intrinsic diameter 1. Thus intrinsic cluster-diameter control at one level gives no MST upper bound. This does not refute high-dimensional scaling. The script checks all 38 connected labeled four-vertex graphs at every selected threshold and exact K_4,K_8,K_16 path examples. Full torus/hypercube bounds remain unresolved in this packet.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "Complete graph K_n",
+        "Distinct weights with path edges first"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-w344-open-problems-20261010:complete-graph-path-mst-reverses-no-diameter-upper-bound",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w344/PROOFS.md",
+        "revision": "2026-10-10 worker result; proof; independent audit pending w346; novelty unverified",
+        "license": "owner-own-work (campaign proof and attributed mathematical source audit)",
+        "excerpt": "For each n>=3 there is a distinct-weight assignment on K_n such that the MST is a Hamilton path"
+      }
+    }
+  ],
+  "relations": [
+    {
+      "from_key": "kruskal-sublevel-intrinsic-diameter-lower-transfer",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.percolation:open-problem-c0087-diameter-of-the-minimal-spanning-tree-on-high-dimensional-tori-and-the",
+      "kind": "cites",
+      "rationale": "New end: \"Let G be a finite connected graph with distinct edge weights, T its minimum spanning tree, and G_p\"; existing end: \"Show that the diameter of the minimal spanning tree (with i.i.d. uniform edge weights) on the torus T_{n,d},\". This result addresses or audits C0087; the cited card identifies its mathematical context and is not used as a proof or a current openness certificate."
+    },
